WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.647
In computer vision, models should adapt gracefully
when image resolution changes,

00:00:05.727 --> 00:00:08.034
a property called scale-equivariance.

00:00:08.114 --> 00:00:10.292
This paper, from Purdue University,

00:00:10.372 --> 00:00:15.455
points out that existing scale-equivariant CNNs are
not truly scale-equivariant,

00:00:15.535 --> 00:00:21.004
because they formulate down-scaling in the
continuous domain and ignore anti-aliasing.

00:00:21.084 --> 00:00:27.069
The authors instead formulate down-scaling directly
in the discrete domain with anti-aliasing,

00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:33.715
and build a new family of deep nets from Fourier
layers that achieves absolute zero equivariance error,

00:00:33.795 --> 00:00:39.200
both in theory and in practice, while staying
competitive on classification accuracy.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.890
Scale-equivariance means that when an object in an
image is resized,

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.312
the network's features should transform consistently,

00:00:47.392 --> 00:00:49.571
and its label should stay the same.

00:00:49.651 --> 00:00:56.221
Recent scale-equivariant convolutional networks
pursue this through weight-sharing and kernel resizing,

00:00:56.301 --> 00:00:59.319
using the same but resized kernel across scales.

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:03.903
The trouble is that these methods are derived in
the continuous domain,

00:01:03.983 --> 00:01:06.098
then discretized when implemented.

00:01:06.178 --> 00:01:10.746
That discretization step introduces a
non-negligible equivariance error,

00:01:10.826 --> 00:01:15.330
so the networks are only approximately
scale-equivariant, not truly so.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:22.165
The key insight is that down-scaling a discrete
signal is fundamentally a signal-processing operation.

00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:26.137
The Nyquist sampling theorem tells us that before
subsampling,

00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:28.572
we must apply an anti-aliasing filter,

00:01:28.652 --> 00:01:32.864
otherwise high-frequency content folds down into
lower frequencies,

00:01:32.944 --> 00:01:36.772
the classic aliasing artifact seen in the
wagon-wheel effect.

00:01:36.852 --> 00:01:38.887
Prior scale-equivariant networks,

00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:42.346
because they were formulated in the continuous domain,

00:01:42.426 --> 00:01:44.653
simply had no place for this filter.

00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:48.048
The authors argue that to be truly scale-equivariant,

00:01:48.128 --> 00:01:52.405
you must formulate the down-scaling directly in the
discrete domain,

00:01:52.485 --> 00:01:55.160
with anti-aliasing built in from the start.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:59.418
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:59.498 --> 00:02:03.376
First,

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:07.335
it formulates the down-scaling operation directly
in the discrete domain as ideal downsampling,

00:02:07.415 --> 00:02:11.293
properly accounting for anti-aliasing.

00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:15.251
Second, it proposes a whole family of deep nets
that are truly scale-equivariant,

00:02:15.331 --> 00:02:19.209
by rethinking every component, convolution layers,
non-linearities, and pooling,

00:02:19.289 --> 00:02:23.167
and re-expressing them as Fourier layers that obey
a simple frequency-dependency rule.

00:02:23.247 --> 00:02:27.125
Third, through extensive experiments on MNIST-scale
and STL-10,

00:02:27.205 --> 00:02:31.084
it shows the model attains an absolute zero
end-to-end scale-equivariance error while

00:02:31.164 --> 00:02:35.042
remaining competitive in classification accuracy
and more data-efficient in low-resource

00:02:35.122 --> 00:02:39.000
settings.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.707
The method starts from a precise definition of
down-scaling: ideal downsampling,

00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:48.113
which first applies an ideal low-pass filter that
zeros out all frequencies above the new Nyquist limit,

00:02:48.193 --> 00:02:52.520
then subsamples.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.927
From this the authors derive their central condition,

00:02:57.007 --> 00:03:01.333
Claim 1: a deep net is truly scale-equivariant if
and only if every output frequency term

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:05.740
depends only on input frequency terms that are
equal or lower.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.147
They then redesign each network component to
satisfy this rule.

00:03:10.227 --> 00:03:14.553
A spatially-local Fourier layer constrains the
kernel so it learns local features while staying equivariant.

00:03:14.633 --> 00:03:18.960
A scale-equivariant non-linearity applies ReLU
frequency-band by frequency-band.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.367
A Fourier pooling layer preserves the same
dependency structure.

00:03:23.447 --> 00:03:27.773
On top of the equivariant feature extractor,

00:03:27.853 --> 00:03:32.180
they add a classifier that predicts once per scale,

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.587
sharing a single MLP across scales through Fourier
padding,

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:40.993
and train it with a hinge consistency loss that
penalizes the model whenever a higher-resolution

00:03:41.073 --> 00:03:45.400
image is predicted worse than its lower-resolution
version.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.857
The authors follow prior work and evaluate on two
benchmarks.

00:03:49.937 --> 00:03:55.854
MNIST-scale is built by randomly downsampling MNIST
digits so that every resolution from

00:03:55.934 --> 00:04:00.829
eight-by-eight up to twenty-eight-by-twenty-eight
is equally represented.

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:05.327
STL10-scale applies the same construction to
natural color images,

00:04:05.407 --> 00:04:09.211
spanning resolutions from forty-eight up to ninety-seven.

00:04:09.291 --> 00:04:12.482
They study performance under ideal downsampling,

00:04:12.562 --> 00:04:17.321
where theory exactly matches practice,
generalization to unseen scales,

00:04:17.401 --> 00:04:20.933
data efficiency at 5k, 2.5k, and 1k training samples,

00:04:21.013 --> 00:04:26.930
and a harder non-ideal downsampling setting where
the anti-aliasing filter is imperfect.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:28.824
The results are striking.

00:04:28.904 --> 00:04:31.310
On MNIST-scale with ideal downsampling,

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:36.092
the model achieves the highest accuracy at
ninety-eight point nine percent,

00:04:36.172 --> 00:04:40.555
the highest scale-consistency at ninety-seven
percent, and, crucially,

00:04:40.635 --> 00:04:42.850
an absolute zero equivariance error,

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:47.313
while competing methods like DISCO show errors
around zero point four.

00:04:47.393 --> 00:04:52.095
The advantage is largest on the harder STL10-scale
natural-image benchmark,

00:04:52.175 --> 00:04:59.618
where the model reaches seventy-three percent
accuracy against roughly fifty-eight percent for the strongest baseline,

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:04.400
a gain of about fifteen points, still with exactly
zero equivariance error.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:10.074
It also degrades gracefully: even under non-ideal
downsampling it remains the best model,

00:05:10.154 --> 00:05:14.920
and in low-data regimes it is the most
data-efficient of all methods tested.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.008
An ablation isolates the consistency loss.

00:05:19.088 --> 00:05:22.797
Across training-set sizes of five thousand,
twenty-five hundred,

00:05:22.877 --> 00:05:26.585
and one thousand samples,

00:05:26.665 --> 00:05:30.373
adding the consistency loss consistently improves
both accuracy and the scale-consistency rate.

00:05:30.453 --> 00:05:34.162
For example,

00:05:34.242 --> 00:05:37.950
at five thousand samples the scale-consistency
rises from ninety-one and a half percent

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:41.738
to nearly ninety-three percent.

00:05:41.818 --> 00:05:45.527
This confirms that the hinge consistency loss is
doing real work,

00:05:45.607 --> 00:05:49.315
encouraging the model to make better predictions as
resolution increases.

00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:53.103
The authors also note that applying the
non-linearity directly in the frequency domain,

00:05:53.183 --> 00:05:56.892
though equivariant, hurt classification,

00:05:56.972 --> 00:06:00.680
which is why they designed the spatial-domain
scale-equivariant non-linearity.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:07.163
The lasting takeaway is that scale-equivariance
should be treated as a signal-processing problem.

00:06:07.243 --> 00:06:09.777
Once you formulate down-scaling as ideal,

00:06:09.857 --> 00:06:16.916
anti-aliased downsampling and require every output
frequency to depend only on equal or lower input frequencies,

00:06:16.996 --> 00:06:21.889
you can build networks from Fourier layers that are
exactly scale-equivariant,

00:06:21.969 --> 00:06:27.753
with provably zero error rather than the small
residual errors that plagued earlier methods.

00:06:27.833 --> 00:06:33.172
And this theoretical guarantee comes at no
practical cost: the model matches or beats

00:06:33.252 --> 00:06:37.506
prior scale-equivariant CNNs on accuracy and is
more data-efficient,

00:06:37.586 --> 00:06:40.120
especially on challenging natural images.
